WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.990
- Is this crazy story.

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:02.640
The "Wall Street Journal"
looked at this and-

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.530
- [Joanna] Previously,
we reported that thieves

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:07.590
are stealing people's
iPhones and passcodes.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.910
- New warning about iPhone thefts

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:10.680
connected to your passcode.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.180
- And then taking over these victims'

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.580
financial and digital lives.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.940
That was chapter one, this is chapter two.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.296
- I went to visit my buddy in Chicago.

00:00:20.296 --> 00:00:21.720
(upbeat music)

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.050
And my phone was stolen.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:24.930
(patrons talking indistinctly)

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:28.410
And the thieves were able
to generate a recovery key,

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:31.170
which would lock me out
of my iCloud account.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:33.360
I'm willing to do anything possible

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.564
just to get my photos and videos back.

00:00:35.564 --> 00:00:37.110
(phone ringing)

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:38.850
- [Support] Thank you for
contacting Apple Support.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.620
- Can't access iCloud account.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.920
- My inbox has been flooded
with notes from people like Greg

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.500
who can't get back into
their Apple accounts

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.410
to access years of
photos, videos, and more.

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:50.850
It's the collateral damage

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:54.840
caused by a hidden iPhone
setting enabled by the thieves.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.117
- The killer has been the recovery key.

00:00:57.117 --> 00:00:59.100
- So, I dug into the secret setting

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.430
and found out that it's flawed, big time.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.770
Thieves committing crimes
like this across the country

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:07.830
use the passcode to
turn on the recovery key

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:10.740
before they steal money
from apps on the phone.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:13.320
This way, the owners
can't remotely get back in

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.650
to wipe the device and use Find my iPhone.

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.800
To understand how thieves
exploit these vulnerabilities,

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.470
think of your Apple account like a house.

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:24.780
The thieves get in and then keep you out

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.480
by changing the main
lock on the front door,

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.550
your Apple account password.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.220
Yes, with the passcode to your iPhone

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.160
they can change the
Apple account password.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.890
It's a major vulnerability in itself,

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:41.190
which I talked about a
lot in that first video.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.530
Now, if a victim wants to get back in,

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.990
they go through account recovery, aka,

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:49.530
the old forgot your
password reset process.

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:52.020
Apple will ask for your
trusted phone number,

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:54.840
provide that and a texted
code, and you should be able

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:58.500
to create a new password
to unlock the front door.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.860
But many of these thieves
put up more barriers.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.840
They change the trusted phone number

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.480
and or create a recovery key.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:08.520
Under Settings, Apple ID,

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:11.190
Password &amp; Security, Account Recovery,

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:13.320
you can turn on a recovery key,

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:16.440
which creates a unique 28 character code.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:17.940
It is now needed to get to the step

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.220
of changing your account password.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.690
If you don't have that code,

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:23.220
Apple says you could be

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.781
locked out of your
account permanently, aka,

00:02:25.781 --> 00:02:28.380
the door can never be opened.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.840
When you call Apple and you tell them

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:34.020
you don't have the recovery
key, what do they tell you?

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:37.410
- To an extent, they told me,
you know, too bad, so sad.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:41.160
There was no possible way to
regenerate the recovery key

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.050
or to get access to my data

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.420
without that specific recovery key.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:47.163
I contacted Apple support,

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.280
many levels tried to escalate that.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:51.570
I went through Apple Legal

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:53.910
who pushed me back to Apple Support.

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:55.500
I've spoken to many attorneys

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:59.220
trying to figure out a course
of action to get my data back.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.230
- When I reached out to
Apple about all this,

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:03.720
a spokesman said, "We
sympathize with people

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.750
who have had this experience
and we take all attacks

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:10.560
on our users very seriously,
no matter how rare.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.270
We work tirelessly every day

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.670
to protect our users' accounts and data,

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.920
and we are always investigating additional

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:20.670
protections against emerging
threats like this one."

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:23.160
The recovery key was supposed
to add extra protection

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.740
to your Apple account from online hackers.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:27.300
But there's a big flaw.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:30.420
Even if someone like Greg already
had the setting turned on,

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.280
a thief with the iPhone's passcode

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.860
could create a new key
right on the device,

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:37.020
which would replace the old one.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.450
Apple designed this as
a convenience measure

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:42.750
for people who misplaced a
previously generated key.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:44.280
Fundamentally, account recovery

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.190
is about proving that
this account is yours.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:49.770
Greg, and many others I've
spoken to have offered

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:53.130
Apple other forms of
identification to prove that.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:55.350
- I've been willing to give Apple

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:59.700
any piece of identification
or verifiable information

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.130
that I am me and this is my account.

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:05.670
Whether or not that be my ID, my passport,

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:07.290
my social security number.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:08.580
- Of course, there's a reason Apple

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:12.210
doesn't keep your social
security number on file, privacy.

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:14.010
There is one thing you can do right now

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:15.210
to prevent this from happening

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:17.430
if your iPhone were ever stolen

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:21.840
Go to Settings, Screen Time,
Use Screen Time Passcode,

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:23.490
and create a passcode that's different

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:25.980
from your main phone passcode.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:30.390
Now, go to Screen Time,
Content &amp; Privacy Restrictions,

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:32.430
then turn on this switch.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:34.980
Scroll down to the
Allowed Changes section,

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:38.820
where it says Account Changes
and select Don't Allow.

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:40.290
Now, a thief wouldn't be able

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:43.560
to get into this area to
change your account password

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:45.780
or turn on that recovery key,

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:48.570
they would need that screen time passcode.

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:51.630
But clearly, there is far
more for Apple to do here

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:53.640
to protect us from crimes like this.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:56.070
The recovery key policies
should be changed

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:57.570
so that it's not the only way

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:00.420
to get back into an account
once it's activated.

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:03.240
And Apple should certainly
restrict the recovery key

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:06.060
from being so easily
deleted or regenerated

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:09.090
by someone who has your
phone and passcode.

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:10.830
And Apple should provide another way

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:13.230
to confirm people are who they are,

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:16.080
possibly by accepting government IDs.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.390
Ultimately, this entire crime is based

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:20.160
around Apple loopholes.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:23.730
A stolen phone, passcode,
and tap of a button

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:27.330
shouldn't mean fighting one of
the world's largest companies

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.010
to get our memories back.

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:31.110
- At this point I'm willing to offer Apple

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.690
whatever it takes to get my
pictures and videos back,

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:37.260
whether it's tens of thousands of dollars,

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:40.143
a retina scan, a DNA
test, whatever it takes.

